package cosmonic:agent@0.4.4;

/// The caller may start child agent sessions and work with them: send them
/// input, wait for their results, cancel them, end them, and see the tree
/// they form. Unstable behind `agent-spawn`.
///
/// Session spawning, not tool loading. A child is an agent session of its
/// own: an agent component (targeting `runnable-agent`) in its own workload,
/// with its own conversation, its own grants and toolsets (nothing
/// inherited), and its own session state. Loading a toolset into the
/// caller's own workload is `tool-loading`, a different power. The two share
/// `toolset-spec`, `admission-error` and the ceiling rules in `spawn-types`.
///
/// Hierarchical. Every session the host starts for a child records its
/// parent session, so sessions form a tree rooted at the session a person or
/// trigger started. The caller works with its own children by session id;
/// it may list its descendants (state only) but acts only on its direct
/// children. A host reads the same tree from its session store to show it
/// (a single-machine host's session list, a command line, a clustered
/// host's session API), and
/// routes a child's approvals to the root session's person, labelled with the
/// child's place in the tree.
///
/// Location-transparent. Nothing here assumes a child runs on the caller's
/// host. Every operation names the child by session id and works through the
/// host's session store, so a child may run on another host, host group or
/// zone, and may move between hosts between runs. `spawn-request.placement`
/// is a hint (see `placement` for when the host ignores it). A session id
/// authorizes nothing on its own. The host records, with each child, the
/// identity of the caller that spawned it (workload incarnation and component
/// name; the session follows from the workload, see `spawn-types`), and acts
/// only for that caller: a sibling component neither sees nor drives another
/// component's children. When that identity ceases to exist (the workload
/// is deleted or recreated), its children end.
///
/// Leases and fencing. A child's run holds an exclusive lease in the session
/// store, with a fencing generation: a counter the store advances when a
/// lease is granted and closes when it expires. The store must be
/// linearizable for leases, generations, spawn tickets and limit
/// reservations (compare-and-swap); a store or zone that cannot give that
/// answers `unavailable` rather than guess.
///
/// - The store, not any one host, decides expiry: the operator's timeout,
///   recommended 60 seconds after the last renewal. Expiry closes the
///   generation at once, so the store rejects any later write, outcome or
///   tool dispatch it mediates for that run, even before a new run starts.
///   Whichever host next reads the child (the parent's, or one serving
///   `children` or `wait`) reports the run as failed: waiters get
///   `run-error.host-lost`, the child's state becomes `failed`, and
///   `child.failure` says why. No host has to be alive for that to happen.
/// - The running host fences itself: on its monotonic clock, once the time
///   since it sent its last successful renewal passes the store's timeout
///   minus a margin (recommended 15 seconds), it stops the child's
///   instances (the agent and its toolsets), so no new model call, tool
///   call or direct effect through any of their imports can start, and
///   cancels what is in flight.
/// - What fencing does not stop. It does not undo or stop effects already
///   under way when the fence fires (a request already sent, a write
///   already issued), and it cannot help when the host's clock misbehaves
///   (a paused virtual machine resumes after the store has expired its
///   lease). Those may overlap a new run, within the child's own reach and
///   never more.
///
/// - Brokered calls carry the generation. Every call the host brokers for a
///   run (model calls, credential-brokered egress, tool dispatch, writes
///   through a host-managed store) carries the run's fencing generation.
///   A broker on the run's own host admits it only while that host's own
///   lease view is valid (the same monotonic clock and margin as the
///   self-fence). A broker elsewhere (a shared model or credential gateway)
///   checks the generation with the store and fails closed when it cannot
///   establish that the generation is open. Direct egress through a
///   component's own grants cannot be fenced this way.
/// - A lock delay before re-placement. After a lease expires, the child's
///   next run is accepted but not started until a re-placement delay has
///   passed (the operator's, at least the self-fencing margin, recommended
///   30 seconds); its progress shows `blocked(placement-delay)`. That gives
///   a cut-off host time to fence itself before a new run starts elsewhere.
///
/// The child's session is durable, so a later `send` starts a new run,
/// placed again, under a new generation.
///
/// What a child's conversation holds after a run that did not finish. Every
/// step the store recorded under the run's generation before the run ended
/// or the generation closed, then a host-written marker saying how the run
/// ended (cancelled, failed, host lost, revoked). Nothing after the
/// generation closed is kept. So a later `send` resumes from the last
/// recorded step; nothing is replayed.
///
/// Stopping. `cancel` cancels a child's run in progress and every run in
/// progress below it. `end` ends the child and every session below it. A
/// person's stop on a session in the host's UI cancels its subtree's runs the
/// same way. A run that ends any other way (done, failed, host lost) leaves
/// the runs it started in its children going: they are durable work a later
/// run can collect, bounded by the tree limits and budgets.
///
/// Forced stop. Cancellation asks cooperatively first. After the host's
/// grace period (the operator's, recommended 15 seconds), each host still
/// running a run in the subtree stops that run's instances, the same way a
/// host fences itself: nothing new starts through any import, calls in
/// flight are cancelled, and metering stops whatever the agent does. A host
/// the request cannot reach is stopped by its lease instead: the store
/// expires the lease (so the run ends `host-lost`), and that host fences
/// itself by its own deadline. `cancel` and `end` resolve when every run in
/// the subtree has stopped or had its lease expire, so at most the grace
/// period or the lease timeout, whichever is longer; dropping the future
/// only stops waiting. A person's stop gets the same deadline. As with any
/// lost host, effects already under way when a run is stopped are not
/// undone.
///
/// Cancellation is a barrier. `cancel`, `end` and a person's stop mark the
/// subtree as cancelling in the session store, atomically, before they
/// cancel its runs. Every `spawn` and `send` (including one already in
/// flight) from a run in that subtree is checked against the mark at
/// acceptance and refused (`denied` or `start-error.denied`), so a
/// descendant cannot start new work that outlives the stop. The mark lifts
/// for a session when its cancelled run has ended.
///
/// Lifetime and limits. A child lives until `end`, until its `max-duration`
/// passes, or until its parent session ends; ending a session ends its
/// descendants. A child with no run in progress holds no instance. Tree
/// limits, always set, operator-adjustable: `depth` (default 3),
/// `concurrent-agents` (runs from acceptance until ended, default 20), and
/// `live-sessions` (child sessions not yet ended, default 50) and
/// `total-agents` (children spawned in all, default 500) per tree. Every
/// root session has a finite token and wall-clock budget (the operator's,
/// which an operator may raise but never remove), so no tree spends without
/// bound. Every run acceptance
/// (`send`, or `spawn` with input) and every spawn reserves its place
/// atomically across the tree. Tokens a child's subtree spends count against
/// the child's `max-total-tokens` and every ancestor's, so a budget bounds
/// everything below it.
///
/// Attachments. A message's stored attachments belong to their session. On
/// `send` (and `spawn` with input) the host copies each stored attachment
/// the parent can read into the child's session and re-issues it there; an
/// outcome's stored attachments are copied into the parent's session the
/// same way and count toward `output.max-bytes`. With
/// `output.structured-only`, an outcome carries no attachments.
///
/// Knowing without polling. `watch` streams changes to the caller's
/// children within a run. Across runs, the host keeps a notice for each run
/// the caller started in a child when it ends (session id, run id, how it
/// ended; never child text) until the caller acknowledges it, and
/// `read-notices` returns them in the caller's next run, so a parent that ended its run while children worked
/// learns what finished without polling. Whether a child's completion may
/// start the parent's next run is a host trigger, not part of this
/// interface.
///
/// Traces. The implementer propagates the trace context of the parent's
/// run (`run-options.trace-parent`) to every child run it starts, on any
/// host, and records spawn, send, cancel, end, lease expiry and revocation
/// as span events, with the session id as the conversation id.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface spawn {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use inference-types.{user-message};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use run-types.{run-outcome};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use session-status.{status};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use spawn-types.{
        spawn-request, admission-error, start-error, run-error, started-run,
        budget-item,
    };
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};

    /// Why a child ended a run as failed, or cannot take another.
    /// Implementer-authored.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-failure {
        /// The host running the child went away mid-run: its lease in the
        /// session store expired. The detail names the host for display.
        host-lost(string),
        /// The child's components could not start.
        start-failed(string),
        /// A budget item is spent, its own or an ancestor's; the child takes
        /// no more runs.
        exhausted(budget-item),
        /// The child's admission no longer fits its ancestors' current
        /// ceilings or policy (an ancestor lost a grant or a toolset, or a
        /// person removed one), so it takes no more runs.
        revoked(string),
        /// A reason not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// One session in the caller's subtree, as the host's session store has
    /// it.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record child {
        /// The child's session id: host-minted, unique, stable for the
        /// session's life, and valid on any host that shares the session
        /// store.
        session: string,
        /// Its parent's session id: the caller's for a direct child.
        parent: string,
        /// Its depth, where a child of a session a person or trigger started
        /// is at depth 1.
        depth: u32,
        /// The request's labels, the caller's own text: set for the
        /// caller's direct children and empty for deeper descendants, whose
        /// labels a child wrote (a person sees them in the host's view).
        labels: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        /// Its session state, derived by the host (`session-status`): needs
        /// input, working, failed, done or idle. None when the host could
        /// not read it.
        status: option<status>,
        /// Why its last run failed or why it takes no more runs, when one
        /// applies.
        failure: option<child-failure>,
        /// Where it last ran, as the host names it for display (a host,
        /// host group or zone). Display only; never an address.
        location: option<string>,
        /// What it and every session below it have spent, metered by the
        /// host, never reported by the child.
        usage: usage-total,
        /// True once the session has ended. The host keeps ended children
        /// listed for a bounded time and number (recommended the 100 most
        /// recent per tree).
        ended: bool,
    }

    /// Spend metered by the host for a session and its subtree.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record usage-total {
        /// Prompt plus completion tokens.
        tokens: u64,
        /// Runs accepted.
        runs: u32,
        /// Model calls made.
        model-calls: u32,
    }

    /// What remains of a budget. Tokens and time are the tightest of the
    /// session's own and every ancestor's, since a subtree's spend counts
    /// against each. None in a field means no limit applies to it below the
    /// root's.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record remaining-budget {
        /// Tokens left.
        tokens: option<u64>,
        /// Wall-clock time left.
        duration: option<duration>,
        /// Runs left for this session, from its own `max-runs` only: runs
        /// are counted per session, not charged to ancestors.
        runs: option<u32>,
    }

    /// The caller's own place in the tree.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record lineage {
        /// The caller's session id.
        session: string,
        /// Its parent's session id; none for a root session.
        parent: option<string>,
        /// Its depth; 0 for a root session.
        depth: u32,
        /// What the caller has left to spend. Every root has finite tokens
        /// and duration.
        remaining: remaining-budget,
    }

    /// A change to the caller's subtree, from `watch`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-event {
        /// A child as it stands when the watch starts. All of these come
        /// first.
        existing(child),
        /// The snapshot is complete; changes follow.
        synced,
        /// A child's state, failure, usage or ended flag changed. Carries
        /// the whole record as it stands now.
        changed(child),
        /// An event not captured above. Safe to ignore.
        other(string),
    }

    /// How a run the caller started in a child ended. Host-authored; never
    /// child text.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant run-end {
        /// It finished with an outcome; `wait` returns it.
        done,
        /// It was cancelled.
        cancelled,
        /// It failed; `wait` returns the error.
        failed,
        /// Its host was lost.
        host-lost,
        /// An ending not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A notice that a run the caller started in a child has ended.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record notice {
        /// Its place in the caller's notice sequence, increasing by one.
        seq: u64,
        /// The child's session id.
        session: string,
        /// The run's id, as `wait` takes it.
        run: string,
        /// How it ended.
        ended: run-end,
    }

    /// Why an operation on a child did nothing.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-error {
        /// No session with that id is a direct child of the caller.
        unknown,
        /// The child's session has ended.
        ended,
        /// The session store could not be reached, or could not answer
        /// consistently. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why `wait` returned no outcome.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant wait-error {
        /// No session with that id is a direct child of the caller, or the
        /// session store could not be reached.
        child(child-error),
        /// No run with that id was started by the caller in that child, or
        /// its outcome is no longer kept.
        unknown-run,
        /// The run failed.
        run(run-error),
    }

    /// A run the caller started in a child.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record sent {
        /// The run's id, minted by the host: what `wait` takes.
        run: string,
        /// Progress, then the outcome.
        started: started-run,
    }

    /// Notices from `read-notices`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record notice-batch {
        /// The notices, oldest first.
        notices: list<notice>,
        /// True when the host dropped unacknowledged notices older than
        /// these, past its retention.
        gap: bool,
    }

    /// What `spawn` started.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record spawned {
        /// The child's session id.
        session: string,
        /// The first run, when `input` was given.
        run: option<sent>,
    }

    /// Check the request against the ceiling, policy and the tree limits,
    /// and start a child session. With `input`, also start its first run.
    /// Resolves once accepted, not once the child is running; a child that
    /// fails to start reports it on its run, its state and `failure`.
    ///
    /// `key` is the caller's idempotency key for this spawn: any string up
    /// to 128 bytes, unique per intended child within the caller's session.
    /// A resend with a key that already admitted a child returns that same
    /// child (and starts no second first run) whatever else it says, for the
    /// life of the caller's session; a different key is a different child.
    /// Key records are bounded by `total-agents`. When identical requests with
    /// different keys share one pending approval, its grant admits the first
    /// key to resend, and the others get a new approval, marked as a repeat.
    /// So one decision admits one child.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    spawn: async func(
        request: spawn-request,
        input: option<user-message>,
        key: string,
    ) -> result<spawned, admission-error>;

    /// Start a run in a direct child: hand it a message and let it work
    /// until it replies or a budget item stops it. Resolves when the run is
    /// accepted. The host first re-checks the child's whole admission
    /// against every ancestor's current ceiling and policy (`denied` and
    /// `child.failure` `revoked` when it no longer fits). `busy` while a run
    /// is in progress there (the caller's, or one a person started by talking
    /// to the child), and after a cancellation until that run has stopped.
    ///
    /// `key` is the caller's idempotency key for this run, as for `spawn`:
    /// a resend with a key that already started a run in that child returns
    /// the same `sent` (the same run id, a fresh view of its progress and
    /// outcome) and starts nothing, whether that run is in progress or has
    /// ended. The host keeps the keys of the 256 most recent runs per child;
    /// an older key starts a new run. A known key whose outcome is no longer
    /// kept still returns the same run, and its outcome resolves to
    /// `run-error.expired`; it never starts the run again. With none, every
    /// call starts a run.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    send: async func(
        session: string,
        message: user-message,
        key: option<string>,
    ) -> result<sent, start-error>;

    /// The outcome of one run the caller started in a direct child, by its
    /// run id: once it ends, or at once if it has ended. Works from any store
    /// and any host that shares the session store, so a caller that started a
    /// run in one run of its own can collect the result in a later one. The
    /// host keeps the outcomes of the runs the caller started in a child,
    /// the 16 most recent at least, until the child's session ends.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    wait: async func(session: string, run: string) -> result<run-outcome, wait-error>;

    /// Cancel a direct child's run in progress and every run in progress
    /// below it: cooperatively, then by force after the host's grace period.
    /// Resolves when the whole subtree has stopped; each run ends
    /// `cancelled`. Nothing to cancel is ok.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    cancel: async func(session: string) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// End a direct child's session and every session below it, cancelling
    /// runs in progress as `cancel` does. Resolves when the whole subtree
    /// has stopped. They take no more runs and stay listed as ended.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    end: async func(session: string) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// The caller's children, oldest first, with their state. With
    /// `descendants`, every session below the caller, each listed after its
    /// parent, so a caller can draw its subtree. Bounded by the tree limits
    /// and the retention of ended children.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    children: async func(descendants: bool) -> result<list<child>, child-error>;

    /// The caller's children (or, with `descendants`, its whole subtree) as
    /// they stand, then each change as it happens, so a caller need not
    /// poll `children`. The stream ends when the caller's session ends;
    /// dropping it stops watching. A reader that falls behind may miss an
    /// intermediate change but always sees each child's latest record.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    watch: async func(descendants: bool) -> result<stream<child-event>, child-error>;

    /// Unacknowledged notices of runs the caller started in its children,
    /// oldest first. Reading clears nothing, so a reply lost before the
    /// caller recorded it is read again. The host keeps at least the 256
    /// most recent unacknowledged notices per session; `gap` is true when
    /// older ones were dropped. A harness reads them at the start of each
    /// run and acknowledges what it has recorded.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    read-notices: async func() -> result<notice-batch, child-error>;

    /// Acknowledge every notice up to and including `through`; they are no
    /// longer returned. Acknowledging again is harmless.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    ack-notices: async func(through: u64) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// The caller's own session id, parent, depth and remaining budget.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    get-lineage: async func() -> result<lineage, child-error>;
}
